266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E (Walmart often sells products in large bulk packs, whereas Walkenhorst tends to sell smaller individual portions. Even when comparing price-per-ounce, this does not account for the natural "economy of scale" price reduction found in larger retail packages.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E (The absence of bulk quantity options in the Walkenhorst catalog could represent a \(monopolistic\) choice constraint in and of itself.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E (One potential \(partial\) solution we considered was to control for product weight in the regression. However, this would have required extracting weight information from Walmart oservations which appear as character strings with heterogeneous and inconsistent patterns. Due to time constraints, we were unable to reliably construct this quantity measure, and therefore did not include it in the analysis.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 165 0 R
/T (Lack of other control variables)
/E (Lack of other control variables)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E (We initially intended to control for the nutritional score and brand effects. However, nutritional score information was unavailable for the Walkenhorst dataset. In addition, brand identification in the Walmart data proved difficult, as brand names appear in product names or description as character strings with inconsistent and heterogeneous formats. As a result, we were unable to reliably construct these control variables and did not include them in the analysis.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
